  { id:'opmcq-c4-1', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"Which condition presents with dryness, burning, and foreign-body sensation, and is commonly associated with autoimmune diseases such as Sjögren syndrome?",
    options:["Blepharitis","Keratoconjunctivitis sicca","Episcleritis","Viral keratitis"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The stem is a definition, not a puzzle: the book prints the disease and the eponym as one label.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.64, under the aqueous-deficient half of the dry-eye aetiology: \"**Sjögren Syndrome ( Keratoconjunctivitis Sicca [KCS] )** — Has an autoimmune aetiology · It is characterised by dry mucous membranes throughout the body.\" The three symptoms the stem lists are the book's own first three dry-eye symptoms on the next page: \"Foreign body sensation or grittiness of the eyes · Burning, stinging, and itching\" (p.65).\n\nSo the question is answered twice over — once by the symptom triad and once by the autoimmune association — and both times by the same two pages.\n\n| Option | What it actually is | Why the stem excludes it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Blepharitis** | Inflammation of the lid margin | It is a *cause* of evaporative dry eye (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.64, \"Eyelid inflammation (blepharitis)\") and the book's own example under \"Treatment of the cause\" (p.65) — but it is lid disease, not an autoimmune ocular-surface disease |\n| **Keratoconjunctivitis sicca** | The ocular surface disease of Sjögren syndrome | The book prints it as Sjögren's own parenthesis |\n| **Episcleritis** | Localised episcleral inflammation | Presents with a red, mildly sore sector of eye, not with dryness — and the book's scleral chapter, not ch.4 |\n| **Viral keratitis** | Corneal infection | Painful, photophobic, unilateral and acute; the autoimmune clause fits nothing about it |\n\n**A**, blepharitis, is the near-miss and deserves its own line, because it *shares* symptoms — burning and grittiness — with the key. What it does not share is the autoimmune sentence. The stem's *\"commonly associated with autoimmune diseases such as Sjögren syndrome\"* is the discriminator, and blepharitis appears in this chapter only as an evaporative contributor and a treatable cause.\n\n**C** and **D** are free options: neither is a dryness disease at all, and neither appears anywhere in `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.4.\n\n⚠️ **Where this is taught matters for revision.** `L9) lacrimal system.` — the lacrimal deck — does **not** teach dry eye. Its own end-of-deck list says so in as many words: dry eye, its classification and Sjögren syndrome are absent, and dry eye survives in that deck only as two passing phrases (slide 9, *\"dry eye ('paradoxical watering')\"*; slide 24, *\"Symptoms of dry eye\"*). Everything in this answer is book, not deck.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Keratoconjunctivitis sicca is the ocular surface disease of Sjögren syndrome — dryness, burning and grittiness on an autoimmune background — and it is taught in the book's lacrimal chapter, not in the lacrimal lecture.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.29 (Ch.4 Q1)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-2', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"A patient complains of foreign body sensation, burning and redness of eye. Dry eye disease was suspected so tear film breakup time test was done and it was 13 seconds. Which of the following applies to that patient?",
    options:["Severe dry eye disease","Moderate dry eye disease","Mild dry eye disease","No dry eye disease"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**One number decides this, and the book prints it twice.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.65, under the signs of dry eye disease: \"**Decreased tear film breakup time (TBUT) — normally more than 10 seconds** (if less, it is an indication of an unstable tear film)\". The tutorial box on the same page repeats it as \"**Normal ≥ 10 sec**\" and gives the method: fluorescein, cobalt blue light, \"time from complete blink to appearance of the first dry spot (black spot)\".\n\n13 seconds is above 10. The test is normal, so **D** — this patient's TBUT gives no evidence of dry eye.\n\n**Note what the stem does and does not establish.** The symptoms — foreign body sensation, burning, redness — are all on the book's dry-eye symptom list (p.65), so the *suspicion* was reasonable. What the question tests is that a normal test does not become a mild abnormality just because someone was suspicious. TBUT is a stability measurement, and above 10 seconds the film is stable.\n\n**A, B and C — the severity ladder — deserve a warning.** *The book grades nothing by seconds.* `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.4 prints exactly one TBUT number, the 10-second cut-off, and no mild/moderate/severe bands anywhere. It uses \"severe\" three times in the chapter and never once attaches it to a TBUT value: \"preservative-free **in severe cases**\" (p.65), \"punctate epithelial erosions ... **in severe cases**\" (p.65), and \"tarsorrhaphy ... **in severe cases** of lagophthalmos\" (p.65). So the three severity options here are distractors built out of a grading scheme the source does not print — which is precisely why the key sits on the fourth. *(Formal TBUT severity bands do exist in dry-eye guidelines; they are **not taken from the course material** and no band would change this answer, because 13 s is normal on any of them.)*\n\n⚠️ **Which source teaches TBUT — measured, not assumed.** It is **not** in `L9) lacrimal system.`; that deck's own absence list records \"TEAR BREAK-UP TIME (BUT/TBUT). Absent\", and its only tear-film numbers are the three layer thicknesses and a marginal tear strip of \"0.2–0.4 mm normally\" (slide 13). Grepping all 29 cached ophthalmology decks case-insensitively for *break-up*, *breakup* and *TBUT* returns exactly one real teaching hit outside the book: `L15,16) Cornea` line 458, where \"Adequate tear film (Schirmer's, TBUT)\" is a **pre-LASIK must-check** — a different clinical use entirely. The 10-second threshold this question turns on exists in `ophthalmology.pdf` p.65 and nowhere else in the course.\n\n**Pairs with `opmcq-c4-10`**, which puts the same test at 7 seconds and asks the mirror question — and which the bank prints TWICE on book p.31, as Q10 and again as Q11, identical down to the typo.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"TBUT is normal above 10 seconds; 13 seconds excludes an unstable tear film however suggestive the symptoms are, and the book grades no severity by seconds.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.29 (Ch.4 Q2)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-3', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"A 6-month-old child presented with epiphora due to nasolacrimal duct obstruction. Pressure over lacrimal sac caused reflux of purulent material from lacrimal punctum. What's the best management for that child?",
    options:["Lacrimal sac massage only","Lacrimal sac massage and probing surgery if no response after 1 year of age","Immediate probing","Incision and drainage"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The child is six months old, and the age is the answer.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.67 prints the escalation as an ordered four-rung ladder, and it is the most exam-shaped passage in the chapter:\n\n> \"2. Infants with congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction: (**very Important**)\n> a. **Digital massage** of the lacrimal sac **with topical antibiotics before the age of one year** (85% resolve within the first year)\n> b. **Nasolacrimal duct probing after the age of one year.** It can be repeated only once if the first attempt was not curative.\n> c. If second probing fails, **silastic intubation** of the lacrimal passages is done.\n> d. **Dacryocystorhinostomy** is indicated if intubation fails.\"\n\nOption **B** is that ladder compressed into one line: massage now, probing later if the massage has not worked by a year. It is the only option that carries both rungs in the right order with the right age attached.\n\n**Why not A, \"massage only\"?** Because it stops the ladder at rung (a) and has no plan for the 15 % who do not resolve. The book's own figure makes the point: \"85% resolve within the first year\" — which is also `L9` slide 21's number (\"spontaneous resolution occurs in approximately 85% within the first year\"). A management that offers nothing to the remaining one child in seven is incomplete, not conservative. **A also drops the topical antibiotics**, which the book attaches to the massage in the same breath and which this child, with purulent reflux, plainly needs.\n\n**Why not C, immediate probing?** The book puts probing *after* the age of one. At six months it is premature. ⚠️ Note that the very next question in this bank, `opmcq-c4-4`, keys exactly this option on a stem that differs from this one by a single word — see there.\n\n**Why not D, incision and drainage?** Incision and drainage is the book's instruction for an **abscess** in acute dacryocystitis: \"For abscess : incision & drainage (Tutorial)\" (p.67); `L9` slide 32 adds the condition — \"if pus points and an abscess is about to drain spontaneously\". This child has purulent reflux through the punctum, which is the opposite situation: the sac is decompressing itself through its natural route. `L9` slide 21 also records that in congenital NLD obstruction \"**Acute dacryocystitis is very rare**\". There is no collection to incise.\n\n**What the purulent reflux actually tells you.** It is a positive regurge test, and the book's tutorial table on p.66 gives it three readings at once: \"+ve regurge : Patent upper lacrimal system · NLD obstruction · Chronic Dacryocystitis\". `L9` slide 12 says the same from the other end — \"Mucopurulent discharge on compression is indicative of a **patent canalicular** system, but with an obstruction **distal to the lower end of the lacrimal sac**\". So the reflux confirms the level of the block; it does not change the age at which you probe.\n\n⚠️ **A live divergence on the conservative window, recorded and not resolved.** Grade Gain's bank sets the window at **six months** — `opqb-t4-141` keys \"Massage with antibiotics up to the age of 6 months\" for a 3-month-old with mucous regurgitation. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.67 sets it at **one year**. `L9` slide 22 describes massage, probing and intubation and gives **no age threshold at all** (its own absence list confirms: \"The AGE at which probing should be performed for congenital NLDO ... Slide 22 describes massage, probing and intubation without giving any age threshold\"). House keys the book's year here. Three sources, three positions; this entry follows the book because House does.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Congenital NLD obstruction is managed by massage with topical antibiotics before one year and probing only after one year — the age, not the character of the reflux, sets the rung.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.29 (Ch.4 Q3)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-4', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"A 6-month-old child presented with epiphora due to nasolacrimal duct obstruction. Pressure over lacrimal sac caused reflux of mucus from lacrimal punctum. What's the best management for that child?",
    options:["Lacrimal sac massage only","Lacrimal sac massage and probing surgery if no response after 1 year of age","Immediate probing","Incision and drainage"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **THIS QUESTION AND THE ONE BEFORE IT DIFFER BY ONE WORD AND CARRY DIFFERENT KEYS.** `opmcq-c4-3` says the reflux is *purulent material* and keys **B**; this one says the reflux is *mucus* and keys **C, immediate probing**. Every other word of the two stems, and all four options in both, are identical. Both stems and both answer lines were re-read from the page image at double magnification to be certain the pair is printed that way and that the keys are not transposed. **They are printed that way. The key is recorded as the bank prints it and is not disputed here.**\n\n**What the printed sources say, quoted so the reader can weigh it.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.67: \"a. Digital massage of the lacrimal sac with topical antibiotics **before the age of one year** (85% resolve within the first year) — b. Nasolacrimal duct probing **after the age of one year**.\" `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 22 describes massage, probing and post-probing intubation and attaches **no age to any of them**; slide 21 gives the same 85 %-within-the-first-year figure. So an immediate probe in a six-month-old is not what `ophthalmology.pdf` p.67 instructs, and `L9` neither instructs nor forbids it because it prints no threshold. That is the whole of the divergence, and it is recorded, not corrected.\n\n**The one reading under which the key stands.** Mucus reflux without pus is the uninfected picture — `L9` slide 21's \"Gentle pressure over the lacrimal sac may cause mucopurulent reflux\" and slide 32's mucocele — and a probe is contraindicated only in **acute infection** (`L9` slide 15: \"It is contraindicated in acute infection\"; slide 32: \"Irrigation and probing should not be performed\" in acute dacryocystitis). So the pair may be testing *when probing is safe* rather than *when probing is due*: pus, do not probe; mucus, probing is permissible. On that reading the two keys are consistent with each other. They are still not consistent with the one-year rule on p.67, and no source in this module prints a rule that makes six months the probing age.\n\n**The distractors, taken in turn.**\n\n- **A, massage only** — rung (a) of the ladder with no exit. The book attaches topical antibiotics to the massage and a probe at one year behind it; \"only\" removes both.\n- **B, massage then probing after one year** — the book's own ladder, and the key of the question immediately above. Here it is not keyed.\n- **C, immediate probing** — the printed key.\n- **D, incision and drainage** — belongs to a pointing abscess in acute dacryocystitis (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.67, `L9` slide 32). This child has mucus refluxing freely through a patent punctum, so there is nothing loculated to drain, and `L9` slide 21 records that acute dacryocystitis is \"very rare\" in this condition anyway.\n\n⚠️ **The conservative window has three different values across this course, and none of them is six months except in the other bank.** Grade Gain: 6 months (`opqb-t4-141`, keyed \"Massage with antibiotics up to the age of 6 months\"). `ophthalmology.pdf` p.67: one year. `L9`: no threshold printed. Recorded for the reader; not resolved here.\n\n**And note the third position this bank itself takes.** `opmcq-c4-21`, four pages later, keys \"Probing is carried out only one time, after 1 year of age\" as the **false** statement — false because the book allows the probe to be repeated once, not because of the age. So House prints the one-year rule as sound in Q21 and keys an immediate probe at six months here.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Mucus rather than purulent reflux marks the uninfected sac, in which probing is not contraindicated — but the book's own threshold for probing congenital NLD obstruction is one year of age, and this key sits earlier than that.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.29 (Ch.4 Q4)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-5', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"A 33-year-old woman presents with ptosis with S-shaped deformity of the upper eyelid. Examination revealed tenderness, edema and erythema of the outer third of the upper eyelid. Which of the following is the best next step in management of that patient's condition?",
    options:["Lacrimal gland scintigraphy","CT orbit","Ophthalmoscopy","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The stem is the book's sign list for acute dacryoadenitis, printed almost word for word.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.69: \"**Tenderness, oedema, erythema, and induration overlying the outer one-third of the upper lid** — **Ptosis with S-shaped deformity of the upper lid.**\" `L10) orbit` slide 31 draws the same picture from the other side: \"Swelling of the lateral aspect of the eyelid overlying the palpebral lobe leads to a characteristic **S-SHAPED PTOSIS**.\"\n\nThe question is what to do next, and the book answers that on the same page. Under **Investigations** (p.69): \"**Computed tomography (CT) scan or magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) of the orbits with contrast**\" — first on the list, ahead of chest X-ray, laboratory tests and biopsy. Hence **B**.\n\n**Why imaging at all, when the diagnosis looks obvious?** Because the book warns on p.68, in a parenthesis of its own: \"(It may be a sign of a more serious pathology underlying the inflammation)\". The lacrimal gland sits in a bony fossa you cannot see into, and the differential behind an inflamed one runs from viral inflammation through sarcoidosis to neoplasm — the book's own aetiology list ends \"...sarcoidosis, or **neoplastic**\". CT settles whether this is a gland or a mass.\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Lacrimal gland scintigraphy** | ⚠️ A test this course never teaches. Grepping *scintigraph* across all 29 cached decks and all 12 cached book chapters returns **zero real hits** — the only occurrences are inside two absence lists (`L9`'s own: \"DACRYOSCINTIGRAPHY (nuclear lacrimal scintigraphy). Absent\"; and ch.4's). `L9` slide 20's lacrimal imaging is **contrast dacryocystography**, and that is a drainage study, not a gland study |\n| **B. CT orbit** | The book's first-line investigation, p.69 |\n| **C. Ophthalmoscopy** | Examines the fundus. The disease is in the anterior superolateral orbit; the posterior pole is not where this pathology lives, and no source lists a fundus finding for dacryoadenitis |\n| **D. None of the above** | Defeated the moment B is correct |\n\n**A is the interesting distractor.** It sounds like a lacrimal test, and it is a real one — but the modality the course teaches for lacrimal *imaging* is dacryocystography, which opacifies the **drainage passages**, and the disease here is in the **secretory** gland. A drainage study asks the wrong question of the wrong half of the system. *(That dacryoscintigraphy is a drainage rather than a gland study is standard radiology and is **not taken from the course material** — the course does not name the test at all.)*\n\n**Chain note:** this vignette runs on into the two questions that follow it — `opmcq-c4-6` asks for the diagnosis and `opmcq-c4-7` asks for the commonest cause, both by back-reference.\n\n⚠️ **Cross-bank:** Grade Gain asks this question with a different patient. `opqb-t4-156`: \"A 19-year-old male has a 2-day history of severe pain in the superolateral orbit, S-shaped ptosis, redness and fever. What is the best initial imaging?\" — key **CT orbit**. Same question, same key, different vignette and a different option menu.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Tenderness and erythema over the outer third of the upper lid with an S-shaped ptosis is acute dacryoadenitis, and the book's first investigation is CT or MRI of the orbits with contrast — because the inflamed gland may be hiding something else.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.30 (Ch.4 Q5)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-6', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"A 33-year-old woman presents with ptosis and an S-shaped deformity of the upper eyelid, with tenderness, edema and erythema of the outer third of the upper eyelid. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Acute dacryocystitis","Chronic dacryocystitis","Acute dacryoadenitis","Dry eye disease"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, what is the most likely diagnosis?\"** The antecedent is the question printed immediately above it (`opmcq-c4-5`), and its vignette has been restated here so the question stands alone. Only the *signs* were carried across, never the answer.\n\n**Two words in the stem settle it: outer third.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.68 opens the section by fixing the location — dacryoadenitis is \"Acute or chronic inflammation of the lacrimal gland (**Appears in the outer one-third of the upper lid**)\" — and p.69 lists the acute signs: \"Tenderness, oedema, erythema, and induration overlying the outer one-third of the upper lid · **Ptosis with S-shaped deformity of the upper lid**.\" `L10) orbit` slide 31 confirms the mechanism: the palpebral lobe of the gland swells under the **lateral** part of the lid, and the lid margin drops laterally while the medial half stays up — an S.\n\n**Acute, not chronic**, because everything in the stem is inflammatory and recent: tenderness, oedema, erythema. The book's chronic form is the opposite picture — \"More common and may present as **painless** enlargement of the lacrimal gland\" (p.69).\n\n| Option | Where the disease actually sits | Why the stem excludes it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Acute dacryocystitis** | The lacrimal **sac**, at and below the **medial** canthus | Wrong corner of the eye. Its signs are \"Pain, redness and swelling over the lacrimal sac **below the medial canthal area**\" (p.67) |\n| **B. Chronic dacryocystitis** | Same medial site, painless | Wrong corner *and* wrong tempo — a painless mucocele (p.68) |\n| **C. Acute dacryoadenitis** | The lacrimal **gland**, superolateral | The stem's exact sign list |\n| **D. Dry eye disease** | The ocular surface | Produces no lid swelling, no erythema and no ptosis; its signs are a reduced TBUT and punctate staining (p.65) |\n\n**The medial-versus-lateral axis is the whole discriminator between the -cystitis and the -adenitis options**, and it is worth fixing once: the **sac** drains, and it lies medially; the **gland** secretes, and it lies superolaterally. `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 7 teaches exactly this contrast as a photograph trio labelled \"Dacryoadenitis | Dacryocystitis | +ve Regurge\".\n\n**Chain note:** `opmcq-c4-7`, printed next, asks the commonest cause of the condition diagnosed here.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"An S-shaped ptosis with tender erythematous swelling of the outer third of the upper lid is acute dacryoadenitis — the gland is superolateral, where the sac is medial.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.30 (Ch.4 Q6)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-7', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"A 33-year-old woman presents with ptosis and an S-shaped deformity of the upper eyelid, with tenderness, edema and erythema of the outer third of the upper eyelid. What is the most common cause of that condition?",
    options:["Staphylococci","Streptococci","Epstein Barr virus","Thyroid eye disease"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, what is the most common cause of that condition?\" — and its antecedent is two links back.** \"The previous case\" is `opmcq-c4-6`, and *that* question's case is `opmcq-c4-5`'s vignette. The chain was followed to its head before rewriting, and **only the signs were carried forward, deliberately not the diagnosis** — naming the disease in this stem would hand over the key of the question printed immediately above it.\n\n**The book ranks the causes, and it puts a virus first.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.68, aetiology of dacryoadenitis:\n\n> \"**Infections** — **Most commonly viral Epstein-Barr virus.** Less common viral causes include adenovirus, varicella zoster, herpes simplex, rhinovirus, cytomegalovirus, or mumps. **Less commonly bacterial** and rarely fungal or parasitic.\"\n\nThat sentence answers the question and disposes of three of the four options at once. **C** is the key.\n\n| Option | Where it sits on the book's list | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Staphylococci** | Inside \"less commonly **bacterial**\" — the book never names a species for dacryoadenitis | Real but not commonest. ⚠️ Staphylococci *are* named as commonest for **dacryocystitis** (p.67), a different disease |\n| **B. Streptococci** | Same — the other organism named for **dacryocystitis**, not for the gland | Not commonest |\n| **C. Epstein Barr virus** | \"Most commonly viral Epstein-Barr virus\" | The key |\n| **D. Thyroid eye disease** | Listed on p.68 under **autoimmune** aetiology, not infective | A genuine cause, but not the commonest and not an organism — the stem asks \"which of the following **organisms**\" |\n\n**A and B are the designed trap, and it is a good one.** The chapter names staphylococci and streptococci exactly once, on p.67: \"The most common organisms are staphylococci or streptococci\" — for **dacryocystitis**. A reader who has fixed that sentence and then meets a lacrimal infection two pages later will reach for it. The two diseases have different commonest causes: **sac = bacterial (staph/strep), gland = viral (EBV)**.\n\n⚠️ **The decks do not rank these, and one of them would mislead you.** `L10) orbit` slide 31 is the only lecture that teaches acute dacryoadenitis at all, and it prints: \"Idiopathic or due to viral (**e.g. mumps, Epstein–Barr, cytomegalovirus**)\". Mumps is printed **first**, but the list is introduced by \"e.g.\" and carries no ranking of any kind — the deck names examples, it does not name a commonest. **The ranking exists only in `ophthalmology.pdf` p.68.** A student revising from the deck alone could reasonably arrive at mumps, which is why this question is a book question. (Mumps is not offered here; it is offered in `opmcq-c4-26`, which asks the same fact without the vignette.)\n\n**D is not a throwaway.** Thyroid eye disease genuinely appears in the book's dacryoadenitis aetiology — \"Autoimmune disorders such as **Thyroid Eye Disease**, Sjogren's syndrome, etc.\" (p.68) — so it is a true cause on the wrong axis of the question. The stem's word \"organisms\" excludes it.\n\n⚠️ **Cross-bank:** `opqb-t4-155` asks the same thing of a 19-year-old man with the same signs — \"What is the most common infectious cause?\", options Staphylococcus aureus / EBV / Herpes zoster / CMV, key **EBV**. Same question, same key, different menu.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The commonest cause of dacryoadenitis is Epstein-Barr virus — the gland's infections are viral, where the sac's are staphylococcal or streptococcal.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.30 (Ch.4 Q7)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-8', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"What is the function of inner mucus layer of the tear film?",
    options:["Prevent / decrease tear evaporation","Keep the ocular surface lubricated","Maintain adherence / stability of tear film","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Every option on this menu is a real sentence from one page of the book — three of them are simply about something else.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.63 prints the tear film as three layers, each with its source and its function:\n\n| Layer | Secreted by | Function, as the book prints it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Outer oily** | Meibomian glands | \"Its main function is to **prevent/decrease tear evaporation**\" |\n| **2. Middle aqueous** | Main lacrimal gland + accessory lacrimal glands | \"It forms the **main bulk** of the tear film\" |\n| **3. Inner mucous** | Conjunctival goblet cells | \"Helps maintain the **adherence and stability** of the tear film on the ocular surface, especially the cornea\" |\n\nOption **C** is the third row, almost verbatim. That is the key.\n\n**Where the distractors come from — this is the point of the question.**\n\n- **A**, \"Prevent / decrease tear evaporation\", is the **oily** layer's function, quoted word for word from row 1. It is correct English and correct physiology attached to the wrong layer.\n- **B**, \"Keep the ocular surface lubricated\", is not a layer function at all — it is the book's description of **basic secretion**, one of the two kinds of aqueous secretion: \"Basic secretion: constant **to keep the ocular surface lubricated all the time**\" (p.63). It belongs to the middle layer's *mode of production*, not to the mucin.\n- **D**, \"None of the above\", falls with C.\n\n**Why mucin does what it does.** The mucin layer sits directly on the epithelium and makes a hydrophobic corneal surface wettable, so the aqueous can spread on it instead of beading off. `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 3 shows exactly this: the mucin layer is drawn **interdigitating with the microvilli** of the epithelial cells, with the labels \"Epithelial cell · Mucin · Microvillus\". Adherence is the mechanical claim, and the picture is the argument for it.\n\n⚠️ **A number worth knowing about but not needed here.** `L9` slide 3 prints layer thicknesses — \"LIPID LAYER 0.1 µm · WATER LAYER 8 µm · **MUCIN LAYER 0.2 µm**\" — while `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy...` slide 56 gives the mucin as **0.02–0.05 µm**; the lipid and aqueous figures agree exactly between the two decks. `ophthalmology.pdf` prints **no thickness at all** for any layer. The divergence is recorded here so nobody re-derives it, but no question in this chapter turns on it — the bank asks functions and sources, never micrometres.\n\n**Siblings in this chapter:** `opmcq-c4-13` asks which layer is the main bulk, and `opmcq-c4-18`, `opmcq-c4-20` and `opmcq-c4-25` ask which structure secretes each of the three layers.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The inner mucous layer, from the conjunctival goblet cells, maintains the adherence and stability of the tear film on the ocular surface — evaporation control belongs to the oily layer and lubrication to basic aqueous secretion.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.30 (Ch.4 Q8)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-9', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"Cyclosporine A 0.05% eye drops may be used in a patient with dry eye disease. Which of the following is the mechanism by which cyclosporine enhances recovery of dry eye disease?",
    options:["It enhances tear film stability","It reduces tear film evaporation","It increase tear film production","All of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The book answers this in a parenthesis, and the parenthesis is the whole question.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.65, under the medical treatment of dry eye disease:\n\n> \"c. **Cyclosporine A 0.05% eye drops (increase tear production)**\"\n\nOption **C** is that parenthesis. (The bank prints it as \"It increase tear film production\", verb agreement and all; kept as printed.)\n\n⚠️ **This is the only drug strength in the entire chapter.** The cache records it explicitly: \"THE ONLY DRUG STRENGTH IN THE WHOLE CHAPTER is Cyclosporine A 0.05%. No other concentration, dose, frequency or duration is printed anywhere in ch.4.\" Everything else the chapter prescribes is named without a number — \"short durations\", \"at bedtime\", \"systemic and topical antibiotics\". So the 0.05 % in the stem is not decoration; it is the one pharmacological number the source commits to.\n\n**Why the other three options fail — read against the book's own four-item medical list (p.65):**\n\n| Option | Which treatment it actually describes | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Enhances tear film stability** | Nothing on the list claims this. Stability is what **TBUT measures** (\"if less, it is an indication of an unstable tear film\", p.65) — an outcome, not a mechanism the book assigns to any drug | p.65 |\n| **B. Reduces tear film evaporation** | The **lipid** layer's job (p.63), and mechanically what **lid tapping or tarsorrhaphy** achieves by \"reduc[ing] the surface area exposed to evaporation\" | p.63, p.65 |\n| **C. Increases tear production** | Cyclosporine A 0.05 % | p.65 |\n| **D. All of the above** | Falls with A and B | — |\n\n**B is the near-miss worth naming.** Reducing evaporation and increasing production are the two ways to raise tear volume, and a reader who knows only that cyclosporine \"helps dry eye\" has no way to choose. The book chooses for you, and it chooses production. The drugs and devices that work on evaporation in this chapter are all non-pharmacological: ointments and gels at bedtime, lid tapping, tarsorrhaphy. The one that works on drainage — **punctal plugs**, \"(to prevent tear drainage)\" (p.65) — is a third mechanism the menu does not offer.\n\n⚠️ **Cyclosporine is a book fact, not a lecture fact — measured.** Grepping *cyclosporin* across all 29 cached ophthalmology decks returns **no teaching hit**: the only occurrence in a deck is in `L6) Conjunctiva`'s own absence audit, which records \"cyclosporin = 0\". In the book it appears in ch.4 (here), ch.5, ch.6 and ch.7. `L9) lacrimal system.` prints **no drug dose, concentration or duration anywhere in its 33 slides** — its own absence list says so.\n\n⚠️ **Cross-bank:** Grade Gain asks the same fact from the opposite direction. `opqb-t4-157`: a 60-year-old man with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis, severe dryness and punctate erosions — \"Which medication used here **increases tear production**?\", options Cyclosporine A 0.05% / Artificial tears / Steroid drops / Punctal plug, key **Cyclosporine A 0.05%**. House gives the drug and asks the mechanism; Grade Gain gives the mechanism and asks the drug. Same sentence of p.65 behind both.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Cyclosporine A 0.05% eye drops work in dry eye by increasing tear production — the only drug strength the book's lacrimal chapter prints, and the only listed agent whose mechanism is secretory rather than anti-evaporative.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.30 (Ch.4 Q9)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-10', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"A 25-year-old man complains of foreign body sensation in the eye coupled with tearing, redness and itching. Tear film breakup time test was performed and it was found to be 7 seconds. Based on the previous data, which of the following would be the most accurate conclusion?",
    options:["Exclude dry eye disease since TBUT is normal","Suspect dry eye disease since TBUT is non-conclusive","Confirm dry eye disease and look for its cause since TBUT is lower than normal","Artificial tear prescription would provide definitive treatment"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **Two source repairs before the medicine.** (1) The bank prints \"**peformed**\"; the stem above restores \"performed\". (2) ⚠️⚠️ **This question is printed twice.** The bank prints this question TWICE on book p.31 — as Q10 and again immediately below as Q11 — identical word for word, option for option, key for key, **including the same typo**. A shared typo is stronger evidence of a reprint than a shared answer. The two printings are held here as ONE entry. Both printings are drafted, because the page prints both; the duplicate is flagged for the hub, not folded here.\n\n**Seven seconds is below the book's threshold, and the threshold is printed twice on one page.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.65, under the signs of dry eye disease: \"**Decreased tear film breakup time (TBUT) — normally more than 10 seconds** (if less, it is an indication of an unstable tear film)\". The tutorial box beside it repeats \"**Normal ≥ 10 sec**\" and gives the method: \"Fluorescein (yellow) · Cobalt blue light (yellow + blue = green) · **Time from complete blink to appearance of the first dry spot (black spot)**\".\n\nSo the film is unstable, and the symptoms — foreign body sensation, tearing, redness — are three of the book's own dry-eye symptoms (p.65). Test and symptoms agree. **C** is the key: confirm, then look for the cause.\n\n**\"And look for its cause\" is not padding — it is the book's third treatment heading.** p.65 prints treatment as \"1. Medical · 2. Interventional · **3. Treatment of the cause: e.g., blepharitis**\", a heading of the same rank as the other two. A confirmed dry eye is a diagnosis that sends you back to a list: the aetiology on p.64 runs from Sjögren syndrome and drugs through thyroid eye disease, blepharitis, screen use, LASIK and contact lenses. Confirming the state without finding what produced it treats symptoms only.\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Exclude, TBUT is normal** | Factually wrong at 7 s. This is the correct answer to `opmcq-c4-2`, where the value is 13 s |\n| **B. Suspect, TBUT non-conclusive** | The book prints a clean cut-off, not a grey zone. 7 s is not borderline on any reading of \"normally more than 10 seconds\" |\n| **C. Confirm and look for the cause** | Matches the threshold *and* the book's own treatment structure |\n| **D. Artificial tears are definitive** | Artificial tears are item **1a** of the *medical* limb (p.65) — symptomatic relief. The book flags their limit in the same breath: \"Long term use of artificial tears with preservatives should be avoided\". Nothing on p.65 calls any treatment definitive |\n\n**D is the option that punishes a reflex.** Artificial tears are the right first drug and the wrong final answer; the book keeps \"treatment of the cause\" as a separate heading precisely so the two are not confused. ⚠️ Note that Grade Gain keys artificial tears as **best first-line management** for an evaporative case (`opqb-t4-151`) — first-line and definitive are different claims, and the two banks are not in conflict.\n\n⚠️ **Cross-bank, on the test itself:** `opqb-t4-162` asks \"**Tear film break-up time** measures:\" with the key **tear film stability** — the property this question's 7 seconds is a measurement of. And `opqb-t4-149`–`151` build a three-question chain on a case with **TBUT = 5 seconds and Schirmer = 14 mm**, which is the pairing House never uses: House asks TBUT alone, twice, at 13 s and 7 s.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A TBUT of 7 seconds is below the book's 10-second normal and, with matching symptoms, confirms dry eye disease — after which the cause must be sought, because treatment of the cause is a heading of its own.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.31 (Ch.4 Q10)' },

  /* opmcq-c4-11 WAS FOLDED, NOT WRITTEN -- 2026-08-24.
     An EXACT within-chapter reprint of `opmcq-c4-10`, immediately below it on the
     same book page (p.31): identical word for word, option for option, key for key,
     INCLUDING THE SAME TYPO ("peformed"). A shared typo is stronger evidence of a
     reprint than a shared answer.
     Q10 is held -- earlier number, same page, fuller explanation (560 w vs 369).
     No `alsoIn`: this is one bank reprinting itself, not two banks printing it.
     Marker unaffected -- the chapter prints no box anywhere, so both carried one.
     Chapter 4 therefore prints 30 numbers covering 29 distinct questions. */
  { id:'opmcq-c4-12', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"All of the following are true regarding acute dacryoadenitis except?",
    options:["It causes pain in superolateral part of the orbit aggravated by eye movement","It causes diplopia especially in medial gaze","It presents with ptosis and S-shaped deformity of the upper lid","It is less common than chronic dacryocystitis"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Three of these four are `ophthalmology.pdf` p.69, printed almost verbatim. The false one is false by a single word.**\n\nThe book's symptoms and signs of acute dacryoadenitis (p.69):\n\n> \"**Pain in the superolateral part of the orbit aggravated with eye movements** · Drooping of the upper lid with difficulty opening the affected eye · Red eye · **Diplopia especially in up gaze or lateral gaze** may be present\"\n> \"Tenderness, oedema, erythema, and induration overlying the outer one-third of the upper lid · **Ptosis with S-shaped deformity of the upper lid** · Conjunctival injection · Proptosis · Fever and malaise may be present.\"\n\n- **A** is the first symptom, word for word. **True.**\n- **B** says \"especially in **medial** gaze\". The book says \"**up gaze or lateral gaze**\". **This is the false statement, and it is the key.**\n- **C** is the second sign, word for word. **True.**\n- **D** is discussed below.\n\n**Why up and lateral, and not medial — the anatomy makes it non-arbitrary.** The lacrimal gland sits in its fossa in the **superolateral** orbit, immediately above and lateral to the globe. An inflamed, swollen gland is a mass in that corner, and the movements that carry the globe *towards* the mass — elevation and abduction — are the ones that meet resistance and produce diplopia. Medial gaze carries the eye **away** from the swollen gland. `L10) orbit` slide 31 supports the same geometry from the other side: \"Enlargement of the orbital lobe may give a slight downward and inward dystopia\" — the mass pushes the globe down and in, which is a displacement away from the gland, not towards it.\n\n**Option D needs its own paragraph, because it is not sourced by either half.** It claims acute dacryoadenitis is \"less common than **chronic dacryocystitis**\" — a comparison across two different diseases from two different sections of the chapter. `ophthalmology.pdf` compares *within* diseases only: p.69 says chronic **dacryoadenitis** is \"**More common**\" than the acute form, and p.67–68 gives dacryocystitis no frequency statement of any kind. `L9) lacrimal system.` teaches dacryocystitis (slide 32) and does not teach dacryoadenitis at all — its own absence list records \"DACRYOADENITIS, acute or chronic ... None are in this deck\". So **no source in this module ranks these two entities against each other.** The key nonetheless treats D as true, which it can do without contradiction because only one option needs to be false. *(That acute dacryoadenitis is an uncommon condition while chronic dacryocystitis is a routine one is general clinical knowledge and is **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n**A reading note on the stem's own logic.** In an EXCEPT question with one clearly printed contradiction, the printed contradiction wins over an unsourced comparison. B is refuted by a sentence you can point to; D can only be argued about.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Acute dacryoadenitis gives superolateral orbital pain worse on eye movement, an S-shaped ptosis, and diplopia in up gaze or lateral gaze — never especially in medial gaze, which carries the globe away from the swollen gland.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.31 (Ch.4 Q12)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-13', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"Which of the following layers is main bulk of tear film?",
    options:["Outer oily layer","Middle aqueous layer","Inner mucus layer","All of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The stem borrows the book's own phrase, so the answer is a lookup.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.63, layer 2 of 3: \"**Middle Aqueous (watery) layer: It forms the main bulk of the tear film.**\" Key **B**.\n\nThe book adds where it comes from and how it is regulated, both worth carrying: \"Secreted from the **main lacrimal gland** as well as the **accessory lacrimal glands in the conjunctiva**\", with \"two types of tear secretion — **Basic secretion**: constant to keep the ocular surface lubricated all the time; **Reflex secretion**: associated with ocular irritation or emotion.\"\n\n**The lecture proves the same point with numbers the book does not print.** `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 3 is a labelled diagram of the tear film in section, and its labels are: \"**LIPID LAYER 0.1 µm · WATER LAYER 8 µm · MUCIN LAYER 0.2 µm**\". Eight micrometres against a tenth and a fifth — the aqueous is roughly forty times the lipid and does not need an argument. The book's figure on p.63 makes the same claim pictorially: the cache records that the diagram \"shows the aqueous band as much the thickest of the three\", though the prose says only \"main bulk\".\n\n⚠️ **The one number the two decks disagree on is not the one this question needs.** `L9` slide 3 gives mucin **0.2 µm**; `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slide 56 gives it as **0.02–0.05 µm**. Lipid (0.1 µm) and aqueous (8 µm) are identical in both decks, and `ophthalmology.pdf` prints no thickness at all. Recorded because it is a live divergence in this module; harmless here, because the aqueous is the thickest layer on either version of the mucin figure.\n\n| Option | Position and share | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Outer oily** | Outermost, from the Meibomian glands; 0.1 µm on `L9` slide 3 | Thinnest of the three. Its job is to stop evaporation, not to provide volume |\n| **B. Middle aqueous** | Between the other two; 8 µm | \"the main bulk of the tear film\" |\n| **C. Inner mucus** | Innermost, on the epithelium, interdigitating with the microvilli; 0.2 µm | A coupling layer, not a volume layer |\n| **D. All of the above** | — | Only one layer can be the bulk; the question is comparative |\n\n**Note the order the two sources use, because it catches people.** The book's prose numbers the layers **outward-in** (oily 1, aqueous 2, mucous 3); the book's own figure and `L9` slide 3 draw them **inward-out** from the cornea (mucin → aqueous → lipid). Same anatomy, opposite reading direction. The aqueous is in the middle either way — which is why the option \"Middle aqueous layer\" is unambiguous however you learned the list.\n\n**Sibling:** `opmcq-c4-8` asks what the inner mucus layer *does*; `opmcq-c4-18`, `opmcq-c4-20` and `opmcq-c4-25` ask which structure secretes each layer.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The middle aqueous layer forms the main bulk of the tear film — 8 µm on the lecture's own diagram against 0.1 for lipid and 0.2 for mucin.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.31 (Ch.4 Q13)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-14', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"All of the following are among the secretory components of lacrimal system except?",
    options:["Lacrimal sac","Goblet cells","Meibomian glands","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ **A transcription note first: this question's key was orphaned.** Book p.31 ends after option D, and \"Answer: A\" is the **first line of book p.32**. It was recovered by reading the next half-page. Three more questions in this chapter break the same way — see `opmcq-c4-19`, `opmcq-c4-24` and `opmcq-c4-29`, which lose option text as well as the key.\n\n**The book divides the lacrimal system in two, and it names the members of each half.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.63:\n\n> \"The lacrimal system is composed of two main parts:\n> **1. Secretory:** Composed of the **main lacrimal gland**, the **accessory lacrimal glands**, **conjunctival goblet cells** and the **Meibomian glands**.\n> **2. Excretory:** Tears drain from the ocular surface through the upper and lower **lacrimal puncta** via the superior and inferior **canaliculi** ... into the **lacrimal sac**. From the lacrimal sac, tears flow through the **nasolacrimal duct** into the nasal cavity, behind the inferior turbinate.\"\n\nThat is the whole question. **The lacrimal sac is named in part 2, not part 1** — it is a reservoir on the drainage route and secretes nothing. Key **A**.\n\n| Option | Which half | What it contributes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Lacrimal sac** | **Excretory** | Nothing. Tears pass *through* it towards the nose |\n| **B. Goblet cells** | Secretory | The inner **mucous** layer (p.63) |\n| **C. Meibomian glands** | Secretory | The outer **oily** layer (p.63) |\n| **D. None of the above** | — | Falls, because A genuinely is the odd one out |\n\n**A useful way to hold it: the secretory half is a list of glands and cells; the excretory half is a list of tubes and a bag.** Every secretory member makes one of the three layers — lacrimal gland and accessory glands the aqueous, goblet cells the mucin, Meibomian glands the lipid. Nothing on the drainage side makes anything.\n\n**The lecture fills in the eponyms the book leaves out.** `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 4 labels the accessory glands on a sagittal diagram — \"**Glands of Krause**\" (circled in red by the lecturer), \"**Glands of Wolfring**\", with Meibomian, Zeis and Moll also labelled — where `ophthalmology.pdf` says only \"the accessory lacrimal glands in the conjunctiva\". `L9` slide 5 adds the goblet-cell distribution, \"denser medially\". Conversely `L9` slide 6 gives the drainage half the measurements the book never prints: **sac 12–15 mm, canaliculus 8–10 mm, nasolacrimal duct 12–18 mm**.\n\n⚠️⚠️ **Cross-bank — this is the closest match in the chapter.** `opqb-t4-142` prints \"The lacrimal **secretory** system consists of the following parts **EXCEPT**:\" with options *The lacrimal gland · The accessory lacrimal gland · The goblet cells · **The lacrimal sac*** and the key on the sac. Same question, same key, three of four options shared in substance — House swaps the two lacrimal-gland options for \"Meibomian glands\" and \"None of the above\". Flagged for the hub as a probable fold; the decision is not mine.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The secretory part of the lacrimal system is the main and accessory lacrimal glands, the conjunctival goblet cells and the Meibomian glands; the lacrimal sac belongs to the excretory part and secretes nothing.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.31-32 (Ch.4 Q14; the key \"Answer: A\" is printed on p.32, orphaned from the question)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-15', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"A 32-year-old man presents with a complaint of excessive tearing. Examination revealed a painless lacrimal sac swelling at the inner canthus. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Acute dacryocystitis","Chronic dacryocystitis","Acute dacryoadenitis","Chronic dacryoadenitis"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**One word carries this question: painless.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.68, chronic dacryocystitis:\n\n> \"Symptoms: Chronic epiphora · Chronic or recurrent unilateral conjunctivitis · **Painless lacrimal sac swelling (mucocele) at the inner canthus.**\"\n\nThe stem is that bullet with a patient attached. `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 32 prints the same sentence: \"A mucocele is usually evident as a **painless swelling at the inner canthus**.\" Key **B**.\n\n**The menu is a 2 × 2 grid, and the stem gives you both coordinates.**\n\n| | **Sac** (medial, drainage) | **Gland** (superolateral, secretory) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Acute** | A — painful, very tender, tense, red, may abscess, fever (p.67) | C — superolateral pain worse on movement, S-shaped ptosis (p.69) |\n| **Chronic** | **B — painless mucocele at the inner canthus (p.68)** | D — painless enlargement of the gland, \"more common\" than acute (p.69) |\n\n- The **site** — inner canthus, and specifically a *lacrimal sac* swelling — eliminates both dacryoadenitis options at once. The gland is in the outer third of the upper lid; the book fixes this on p.68 (\"Appears in the outer one-third of the upper lid\").\n- The **absence of pain** eliminates the acute sac option. Acute dacryocystitis is defined by pain: \"Pain, redness and swelling over the lacrimal sac below the medial canthal area ... Very tender and tense swelling with overlying skin erythema\" (p.67).\n\n**Why there is a mucocele at all.** Chronic dacryocystitis follows a blocked nasolacrimal duct — \"usually secondary to Obstruction of the nasolacrimal duct\" (p.68) — so the sac fills with mucus it cannot pass on, and distends. That also explains the tearing the patient complains of, and it predicts the sign that confirms the diagnosis at the slit lamp: \"pressure on the lacrimal sac may result in **reflux of mucopurulent discharge** ... through the punctum (**positive regurge test**)\" (p.68). `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 7 teaches exactly this trio as photographs — \"Dacryoadenitis | Dacryocystitis | **+ve Regurge**\".\n\n**D, chronic dacryoadenitis, is the option most likely to be picked by someone reading only the word \"chronic\".** It is a real entity and the book calls it \"**More common**\" than the acute form (p.69) — but it presents as painless enlargement of the *gland*, superolaterally, and it does not produce a swelling at the inner canthus or excessive tearing.\n\n**Chain note:** `opmcq-c4-16` back-references this vignette and asks for the treatment. **Mirror:** `opmcq-c4-17` gives the same medial swelling made **painful and red**, and keys the acute form.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A painless lacrimal sac swelling at the inner canthus with epiphora is a mucocele of chronic dacryocystitis — painless separates it from the acute form, and the medial site separates it from dacryoadenitis.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.32 (Ch.4 Q15)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-16', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"A 32-year-old man presents with excessive tearing and a painless lacrimal sac swelling at the inner canthus. Which of the following is the best line of management?",
    options:["Warm compresses and nasal decongestants","Systemic and topical antibiotics","Delayed DCR","Immediate DCR"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, which of the following is the best line of management?\"** The antecedent is `opmcq-c4-15`, one link back, and its vignette has been restated above. Only the **signs** were carried across — the diagnosis was deliberately left out, so this question does not answer the one printed before it.\n\n**The book gives chronic dacryocystitis exactly one treatment, in one line.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.68, closing the chronic section: \"**Treatment: Dacryocystorhinostomy (DCR)**\". No compresses, no antibiotics, no waiting period. Key **D**.\n\n**Why nothing is deferred.** There is no infection to settle first. The chronic picture is a mechanical one — a nasolacrimal duct that has closed, and a sac that has distended behind it into a mucocele. Antibiotics do not open a duct, and time will not either. DCR is the operation that bypasses the block: the book defines it on p.67 as \"surgically connecting the lacrimal sad [sic] and the middle meatus of the nasal cavity with placement of either temporary or permanent tubes\". `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 29 lists the two routes — \"**External DCR · Endoscopic DCR**\" — and gives no other treatment for acquired nasolacrimal duct obstruction either.\n\n| Option | Whose treatment it actually is | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Warm compresses and nasal decongestants** | **Acute** dacryocystitis, first line | p.67 |\n| **B. Systemic and topical antibiotics** | **Acute** dacryocystitis, second line | p.67 |\n| **C. Delayed DCR** | Also **acute** dacryocystitis — \"Once the infection resolves, **consider** dacryocystorhinostomy DCR\" | p.67 |\n| **D. Immediate DCR** | Chronic dacryocystitis | p.68 |\n\n**A, B and C are one coherent wrong answer: they are the acute protocol in order.** That is what makes the question worth asking — the reader who has not separated the two forms will pick the sequence rather than the operation, and the sequence is right for the other disease. The rule to carry: **acute = settle the infection, then consider DCR; chronic = DCR.**\n\n**C is the sharpest distractor**, because \"delayed DCR\" is not wrong medicine — it is precisely what `ophthalmology.pdf` p.67 instructs *after* acute infection. The delay exists to avoid operating on inflamed, infected tissue. Here there is no inflammation to wait out; the swelling is painless and the skin is not described as red, so nothing is gained by postponing.\n\n**A practical footnote from the lecture, on what must *not* be done in the acute case for contrast:** `L9` slide 32 — \"**Irrigation and probing should not be performed**\" — and slide 15, \"It is contraindicated in acute infection\". Neither restriction applies to this patient.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Chronic dacryocystitis is treated by dacryocystorhinostomy without delay — compresses, decongestants, antibiotics and a deferred DCR are the acute protocol, not the chronic one.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.32 (Ch.4 Q16)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-17', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"A 33-year-old female patient presents to the ophthalmologist with a painful red swelling localized over the medial canthal area. Which of the following is the most suitable diagnosis for such a swelling?",
    options:["Acute dacryocystitis","Acute dacryoadenitis","Chronic dacryocystitis","Chronic dacryoadenitis"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Site plus tempo, and the book prints both.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.67, acute dacryocystitis:\n\n> \"Symptoms: **Pain, redness and swelling over the lacrimal sac below the medial canthal area** · Epiphora · Fever may be present.\n> Signs: **Very tender and tense swelling with overlying skin erythema** · It can progress to abscess formation · Preseptal cellulitis may be present.\"\n\nThe stem is the first bullet with the words rearranged. Key **A**. `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 32 says the same: \"the subacute onset of pain in the **medial canthal area**, associated with epiphora. A very tender, tense **red swelling** develops at the medial canthus, commonly progressing to abscess formation.\"\n\n**The two coordinates, taken separately:**\n\n- **Medial canthal area** → the lacrimal **sac**, so a *dacryocystitis*. The lacrimal **gland** is superolateral, \"in the outer one-third of the upper lid\" (p.68), so both dacryoadenitis options are wrong on location before tempo is even considered.\n- **Painful and red** → **acute**. The chronic sac disease is a \"**Painless** lacrimal sac swelling (mucocele) at the inner canthus\" (p.68), and chronic gland disease is \"**painless** enlargement of the lacrimal gland\" (p.69). Pain excludes both chronic options.\n\n| Option | Site | Tempo | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A. Acute dacryocystitis** | Medial ✓ | Painful ✓ | The key |\n| **B. Acute dacryoadenitis** | Superolateral ✗ | Painful ✓ | Right tempo, wrong corner — it gives superolateral pain and an S-shaped ptosis (p.69) |\n| **C. Chronic dacryocystitis** | Medial ✓ | **Painless** ✗ | Right corner, wrong tempo — this is `opmcq-c4-15` |\n| **D. Chronic dacryoadenitis** | Superolateral ✗ | Painless ✗ | Wrong on both |\n\n⚠️ **Note the option order changed between questions.** `opmcq-c4-15` prints the same four diagnoses in the order acute-cyst / chronic-cyst / acute-aden / chronic-aden; this question prints acute-cyst / **acute-aden** / chronic-cyst / chronic-aden. The lists are transcribed exactly as printed in both, because the answer is an index into them.\n\n**What follows clinically, and it is why this diagnosis matters more than a label.** The book warns that acute dacryocystitis \"can progress to **abscess formation**\" and that \"**Preseptal cellulitis** may be present\" (p.67). `L9` slide 12 adds a practical instruction for the examination itself: \"**In acute dacryocystitis, palpation is painful and should be avoided**\" — so the regurge test that confirms the chronic form is not to be performed here.\n\n⚠️ **Cross-bank:** `opqb-t4-154` uses this exact vignette — \"a 42-year-old woman presents with painful, red swelling **below the medial canthus**, very tender, with fever\" — but asks **best immediate management** rather than the diagnosis, keying \"Systemic antibiotics + warm compress\". House asks management separately, at `opmcq-c4-30`. Same clinical picture split into two questions across two banks.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A painful red tender swelling at the medial canthus is acute dacryocystitis — the medial site names the sac, the pain names the acute form, and palpation should be avoided.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.32 (Ch.4 Q17)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-18', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"Which of the following is the structure responsible for secreting inner mucus layer of tear film?",
    options:["Meibomian glands","Lacrimal glands","Goblet cells","All of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Direct book recall.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.63, layer 3 of 3: \"**Inner mucous layer: Secreted by the conjunctival goblet cells** · Helps maintain the adherence and stability of the tear film on the ocular surface, especially the cornea.\" Key **C**.\n\n**This is one of three sibling questions in the chapter that share the same four options in the same order** and differ only in which layer they name. Answering any of them is answering the same table:\n\n| Layer | Secreted by | This bank's question |\n|---|---|---|\n| Outer **oily** | **Meibomian glands** | `opmcq-c4-25`, keyed A |\n| Middle **aqueous** | **Lacrimal glands** (main + accessory) | `opmcq-c4-20`, keyed B |\n| Inner **mucous** | **Goblet cells** | this one, keyed C |\n\nThe menu is built so that each layer's true source is a distractor for the other two — there is no partial credit for knowing that all three structures secrete *something*.\n\n**D, \"All of the above\", is false and worth a sentence.** The three sources are not interchangeable and the tear film is not a mixture: it is stratified, each layer made by a different tissue in a different place. `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 3 draws it — lipid outermost, water in the middle, and the mucin innermost \"**interdigitating with the microvilli**\" of the epithelium.\n\n**Where the goblet cells actually are.** They are in the **conjunctival epithelium**, not in a discrete gland — which is why the book calls them \"conjunctival goblet cells\" and lists them among the secretory components alongside the glands (p.63). `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 5 shows their distribution on a diagram captioned \"Distribution of goblet cells on the conjunctiva with eyelids everted\", labelled \"Lateral\" and \"Medial\", with the stippling \"visibly denser medially\". That density gradient is figure-only teaching — it is printed on no slide in words and appears nowhere in the book.\n\n**Clinical hook.** Goblet cell loss is why cicatrising conjunctival disease dries the eye: no mucin, no wettable surface, and the aqueous beads off however much of it there is. The book approaches this from the aetiology side rather than the histology side, listing \"Goblet cell destruction\" nowhere in ch.4 but naming vitamin A deficiency among dry-eye causes on p.64. *(The mechanism — mucin deficiency causing surface non-wetting — is standard physiology and is **not taken from the course material**, which states the goblet cells' function without explaining the failure mode.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The inner mucous layer of the tear film is secreted by the conjunctival goblet cells — Meibomian glands make the oily layer and the lacrimal glands the aqueous.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.32 (Ch.4 Q18)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-19', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"Dacryocystorhinostomy (DCR) is indicated in all of the following conditions except?",
    options:["Epiphora","Dacryocystitis","Dacryoadenitis","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **Transcription note: this question breaks across a page.** Book p.32 ends after option \"B. Dacryocystitis\"; **options C and D and the key all open book p.33**. Reading only the page the question starts on would have produced a two-option question with no answer. Recovered from the next half-page.\n\n**DCR bypasses a blocked drainage system, so it is indicated in drainage disease and in nothing else.** Trace where the book actually prescribes it:\n\n| Condition | Does the book prescribe DCR? | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Epiphora** | **Yes** — item 3 of the epiphora treatment list, and item (d) of the congenital NLD obstruction ladder (\"Dacryocystorhinostomy is indicated if intubation fails\") | p.67 |\n| **Dacryocystitis** | **Yes**, both forms — acute: \"Once the infection resolves, **consider dacryocystorhinostomy DCR**\"; chronic: \"**Treatment: Dacryocystorhinostomy (DCR)**\" | p.67, p.68 |\n| **Dacryoadenitis** | **No.** The treatment list is symptomatic care for viral disease, systemic broad-spectrum antibiotics for bacterial, oral corticosteroids for idiopathic, and treating the underlying disorder if chronic. **No operation of any kind appears** | p.69 |\n\nKey **C**.\n\n**The reason is structural, not a list to memorise.** DCR is an anastomosis between the lacrimal **sac** and the nose — the book's own definition is \"surgically connecting the lacrimal sad [sic] and the middle meatus of the nasal cavity with placement of either temporary or permanent tubes\" (p.67). Dacryoadenitis is inflammation of the lacrimal **gland**, which sits at the *other end* of the system, superolaterally, and produces tears rather than draining them. An operation on the drainage route cannot treat a disease of the secretory gland. Nothing in dacryoadenitis is obstructed.\n\n**D, \"None of the above\", fails** because C is a genuine exception — it is not the case that all three are indications.\n\n**What `L9) lacrimal system.` adds.** Slide 29 lists DCR (external and endoscopic) as the treatment of **acquired nasolacrimal duct obstruction**; slide 22's post-probing ladder for the congenital form is cut off by the slide edge before DCR is named, so the operation's place in congenital disease comes from the book alone. `L9` also names a *different* operation for a *different* level of block — \"**Conjunctivodacryocystorhinostomy** and the insertion of a toughened glass (**Lester Jones**) tube\" for canalicular obstruction (slide 27). Neither is in the book at all, and neither is offered here.\n\n⚠️ **Cross-bank, and this one must NOT be folded on its shape.** `opqb-t4-160` asks the same *kind* of question — \"One of the following is **not** an indication for dacryo-cysto-rhinostomy\" — but its menu is *lacrimal fistula · acute dacryocystitis · chronic dacryocystitis · mucocele of the lacrimal sac*, and its key is **acute dacryocystitis** (excluded because you do not operate on an actively infected sac; you settle it first, p.67). House's menu contains no overlapping distractor and keys dacryoadenitis. **Two different questions with two different correct answers.** Flagged for the hub precisely so the resemblance is not mistaken for a reprint.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"DCR is an operation on the lacrimal drainage route, so it is indicated in epiphora and in dacryocystitis but never in dacryoadenitis, which is disease of the secretory gland.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.32-33 (Ch.4 Q19; options C-D and the key are printed on p.33, orphaned from the stem)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-20', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"Which of the following is the structure responsible for secreting middle watery layer of tear film?",
    options:["Meibomian glands","Lacrimal glands","Goblet cells","All of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Direct book recall.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.63, layer 2 of 3: \"**Middle Aqueous (watery) layer** · It forms the main bulk of the tear film · **Secreted from the main lacrimal gland as well as the accessory lacrimal glands in the conjunctiva.**\" Key **B**.\n\n**Note the plural, because the book puts weight on it.** The key is \"Lacrimal gland**s**\", and that covers two things: the **main** lacrimal gland in its fossa superolaterally, and the **accessory** lacrimal glands scattered in the conjunctiva. The book names the second group without naming its members. `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 4 supplies the eponyms as figure labels on a sagittal diagram of the lids and conjunctival sac: \"**Glands of Krause**\" — circled in red by the lecturer, and labelled again inferiorly — and \"**Glands of Wolfring**\", alongside Meibomian, Zeis and Moll. Krause and Wolfring appear **nowhere in `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.4**; the book's cache records that \"Krause\" is absent from the chapter entirely and \"Wolfring\" survives only as a label in the eyelid cross-section of ch.5.\n\n**Sibling table** — the same four options, in the same order, serve three questions:\n\n| Layer | Source | Question |\n|---|---|---|\n| Outer oily | Meibomian glands | `opmcq-c4-25`, key A |\n| Middle aqueous | Lacrimal glands | this one, key B |\n| Inner mucous | Goblet cells | `opmcq-c4-18`, key C |\n\n**A, Meibomian glands**, make the **oily** layer (p.63) — the outermost, and the thinnest at 0.1 µm on `L9` slide 3. **C, goblet cells**, make the **mucous** layer. **D, all of the above**, is false: the film is stratified, with a different tissue behind each stratum.\n\n**Two kinds of aqueous secretion, both on the same page, worth carrying.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.63: \"**Basic secretion**: constant to keep the ocular surface lubricated all the time\" and \"**Reflex secretion**: associated with ocular irritation or emotion\". That second line explains the paradox the epiphora section leans on — a **dry** eye can **water**, because irritation drives reflex lacrimation. The book lists it among the causes of epiphora as \"Dry eye (reflex lacrimation)\" (p.66) and `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 9 calls it by name: \"Hypersecretion secondary to anterior segment disease such as dry eye (**'paradoxical watering'**)\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The middle aqueous layer is secreted by the lacrimal glands — the main gland plus the accessory glands of Krause and Wolfring in the conjunctiva — and it has both a basic and a reflex component.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.33 (Ch.4 Q20)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-21', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"All of the following are true regarding treatment of congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction except?",
    options:["Digital massage is usually the 1st line of treatment","Probing is carried out only one time, after 1 year of age","Silastic intubation is resorted to in case of failure of probing","DCR is usually the last resort in case of intubation failure","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️ **Five options here, A–E, where the chapter's other questions print four.** Re-read from the page to confirm that \"E. None of the above\" is an option and not the next question's stem. `opmcq-c4-27` is the only other five-option question in the chapter.\n\n**This question is `ophthalmology.pdf` p.67's four-rung ladder printed back to the reader, with one rung altered.** The source, in full:\n\n> \"2. Infants with congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction: (**very Important**)\n> a. **Digital massage** of the lacrimal sac with topical antibiotics before the age of one year (85% resolve within the first year)\n> b. Nasolacrimal duct **probing after the age of one year. It can be repeated ONLY ONCE if the first attempt was not curative.**\n> c. **If second probing fails, silastic intubation** of the lacrimal passages is done.\n> d. **Dacryocystorhinostomy is indicated if intubation fails.**\"\n\nMap the options onto it:\n\n| Option | Rung | True? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A.** Digital massage is usually the 1st line | (a) | **True** — verbatim |\n| **B.** Probing carried out **only one time**, after 1 year | (b), altered | **FALSE — the key** |\n| **C.** Silastic intubation after failure of probing | (c) | **True** |\n| **D.** DCR is the last resort after intubation failure | (d) | **True** |\n| **E.** None of the above | — | Falls, because B is false |\n\n**Where B goes wrong is subtle and deliberate: it is right about the age and wrong about the number.** The book puts probing after one year — so \"after 1 year of age\" is correct — but it explicitly allows a **second** attempt: \"It can be repeated only once if the first attempt was not curative.\" Rung (c) then depends on that second attempt existing: \"If **second** probing fails, silastic intubation ... is done.\" A reader who deletes the repeat has no way to reach rung (c) as the book writes it. So B contradicts two consecutive sentences, not one.\n\n**Why massage first, and why it usually suffices.** `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 21: \"The lower end of the nasolacrimal duct, in the region of the **valve of Hasner**, is the last portion of the lacrimal drainage system to canalize\", and \"Epiphora affects at least **20 % of neonates**, but spontaneous resolution occurs in approximately **85 % within the first year**\" — a number the book repeats on p.67. Slide 22 gives the mechanism massage is meant to exploit: \"Massage of the lacrimal sac has been suggested as a means of **rupturing a membranous obstruction by hydrostatic pressure**\", and describes probing as passing \"a fine wire via the canalicular system and nasolacrimal duct to **disrupt the obstructive membrane at the valve of Hasner**, followed by irrigation to confirm the patency\".\n\n⚠️ **`L9` gives no age for any of this** — its own absence list records that slide 22 \"describes massage, probing and intubation **without giving any age threshold**\", and that slide 21's 85 %-in-the-first-year is its only timing figure. So the one-year rule that makes option B's second clause correct comes **only from `ophthalmology.pdf` p.67**.\n\n⚠️ **And the conservative window is a live three-way divergence.** Grade Gain sets it at **six months** (`opqb-t4-141`); the book at **one year**; `L9` prints none. ⚠️⚠️ **This bank is not internally consistent about it either**: `opmcq-c4-4`, four questions into the chapter, keys \"Immediate probing\" for a **6-month-old**. Here it treats \"after 1 year of age\" as the sound half of a statement. Recorded on both entries; neither key is disputed.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The congenital NLD obstruction ladder is massage before one year, probing after one year and repeatable once, then silastic intubation, then DCR — the probe is not a single-attempt procedure.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.33 (Ch.4 Q21)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-22', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"Which of the following is the most important line of management of dry eye disease?",
    options:["Artificial tears","Interventions as punctal plug insertion","Treatment of the cause","Topical steroids"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The book's treatment of dry eye is printed as three numbered headings, and the key is the third of them.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.65:\n\n> \"**1. Medical:** a. Artificial tears (with preservatives or preservative-free in severe cases) ... b. Longer acting agents such as artificial tear gel and ointments at bedtime. c. Cyclosporine A 0.05% eye drops (increase tear production). d. Topical steroids for short durations help control the inflammatory aspect of the disease.\n> **2. Interventional:** a. Punctal plugs insertion (to prevent tear drainage). b. Lid tapping overnight or tarsorrhaphy ... in severe cases of lagophthalmos.\n> **3. Treatment of the cause:  e.g., blepharitis\"**\n\nThe four options are one item from heading 1 (A), one from heading 2 (B), heading 3 itself (C), and a second item from heading 1 (D). Key **C**.\n\n**The argument for the key is structural rather than quoted.** ⚠️ *The book does not print the word \"most important\" anywhere in this section*, so nothing here can be lifted verbatim in support. What the book does is give \"Treatment of the cause\" **the same rank as Medical and Interventional** — a numbered heading of its own, not a bullet inside one — and dry eye disease is a state produced by something else. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.64 devotes a full page to the aetiology, in three groups: aqueous deficiency (Sjögren, non-Sjögren — allergies, ageing, pregnancy, thyroid, drugs, chemical injury, eye surgery, reduced corneal sensation, diabetes, vitamin A deficiency), evaporative (thyroid eye disease, blepharitis, infrequent blinking at screens, environment, neurological disease, exposure keratitis, mask-associated dry eye), and mixed. Every item on that list has its own treatment, and several are curable in a way the dryness is not.\n\n| Option | What it does | Ceiling |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Artificial tears** | Replaces volume | Symptomatic; the book warns preserved drops \"should be avoided\" long term |\n| **B. Punctal plugs** | Retains what is produced — \"to prevent tear drainage\" | Interventional, and it treats the consequence |\n| **C. Treatment of the cause** | Removes the driver | The key |\n| **D. Topical steroids** | \"help control the **inflammatory aspect** of the disease\" — the book's own limiting phrase, and \"for **short durations**\" | Adjunct, time-limited |\n\n**D deserves the warning attached to it.** The book qualifies steroids twice in eleven words — *inflammatory aspect*, *short durations* — which is as close as ch.4 comes to naming a risk. A treatment that must be stopped cannot be the most important line in a chronic disease.\n\n⚠️ **Cross-bank, and the two banks key adjacent ideas differently.** `opqb-t4-151` gives an evaporative case (TBUT 5 s, Schirmer 14 mm) and asks \"**Best first-line management?**\", keying **artificial tears** — House's option A. There is no contradiction: *first-line* and *most important* are different questions, and the book supports both readings on the same page. Flagged so the hub sees the pair.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Dry eye treatment has three headings of equal rank — medical, interventional, and treatment of the cause — and the third is the only one that removes the driver rather than managing the consequence.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.33 (Ch.4 Q22)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-23', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"All of the following are true regarding epiphora except?",
    options:["DCR may be used as a line of management","Probing is curative in children and adults","It’s either due to increased production or decreased drainage of tears","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️ **Source misprint repaired: the bank prints option D as \"Non of the above\"** — one letter short of \"None\". The option is given above with the spelling corrected, and what the bank printed is recorded here. (`opmcq-c4-19` and `opmcq-c4-24`, on the facing pages, both print \"None\" correctly.)\n\n**The key is a single sentence the book sets apart on its own line.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.67, immediately after the epiphora treatment list: \"**N.B: Probing is not curative in adults.**\" Option B claims it is curative in children **and adults**. False, and the key is **B**.\n\n**Why probing works in a child and not in an adult — the two obstructions are different things.** In the infant, the block is a **membrane**: `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 21 explains that \"the lower end of the nasolacrimal duct, in the region of the **valve of Hasner**, is the last portion of the lacrimal drainage system to canalize\", and slide 22 describes probing as passing a fine wire \"to **disrupt the obstructive membrane** at the valve of Hasner\". A membrane can be broken once and stay broken. In the adult, the block is **stenosis of the duct itself** — `L9` slide 29 lists the causes of acquired nasolacrimal duct obstruction as \"**Idiopathic stenosis – by far the most common** · Naso-orbital trauma, including nasal and sinus surgery · Granulomatous disease such as sarcoidosis · Infiltration by nasopharyngeal tumours\", and gives the treatment as external or endoscopic **DCR**. A stenosed duct dilated by a probe restenoses; that is why the adult needs a bypass rather than a passage.\n\n**The two true options.**\n\n- **A. DCR may be used as a line of management.** True — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.67 lists \"3. **Dacryocystorhinostomy (DCR)**\" as an epiphora treatment in its own right, and as rung (d) of the congenital ladder.\n- **C. It's either due to increased production or decreased drainage.** True, and it is the book's own two-part structure. p.66: \"Causes: **1. Overproduction of tears** ... **2. Decreased drainage of tears**\". `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 9 prints the identical split: \"There are two mechanisms: 1) **Hypersecretion** secondary to anterior segment disease such as dry eye ('paradoxical watering') or inflammation. 2) **Defective drainage** — lid malposition ... obstruction at any point along the drainage system ... lacrimal pump failure.\"\n\n**D falls** because B is genuinely false.\n\n**A note on what \"epiphora\" means, since C rests on it.** The book defines it as \"Excessive watering of the eye with the **overflow of tears at the lid margin** as a result of insufficient tear drainage\" (p.66); `L9` slide 9 as \"the overflow of tears at the eyelid margin\". The overflow is the sign; either too much tear or too little drainage will produce it, which is exactly why the diagnostic work-up in this topic is about **finding the level**, not about confirming the symptom.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Epiphora is overproduction or defective drainage, and DCR is a legitimate treatment — but probing cures the infant's membranous block at the valve of Hasner and is explicitly not curative in adults.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.33 (Ch.4 Q23; option D is printed \"Non of the above\")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-24', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"All of the following conditions can cause epiphora except?",
    options:["Dry eye disease","Entropion","Ectropion","None of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"⚠️ **Transcription note: this question breaks across a page.** Book p.33 ends after option \"A. Dry eye disease\"; **options B, C and D and the key all open book p.34**. Recovered by reading the next half-page — one of four questions in this chapter that break this way.\n\n**The key is D, which means all three named conditions do cause epiphora — and the book puts each of them on its causes list, in three different places.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.66:\n\n| Condition | Which limb of the book's causes list | The book's words |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Dry eye disease** | **1. Overproduction** of tears, secondary to anterior segment disease | \"**Dry eye (reflex lacrimation)**\" |\n| **B. Entropion** | **1. Overproduction** — under ocular surface *irritation* | \"Ocular surface irritation ( **entropion** - trichiasis)\" |\n| **C. Ectropion** | **2. Decreased drainage** — under punctal malposition | \"Malposition of the lacrimal puncta (**e.g. ectropion**)\" |\n\nSo the three options are not variations on one mechanism: dry eye and entropion water the eye by **making too many tears**, ectropion by **failing to drain the ones there are**. `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 9 splits them identically — \"1) **Hypersecretion** secondary to anterior segment disease such as **dry eye** ('paradoxical watering') or inflammation. 2) **Defective drainage** — **Lid Malposition (e.g. ectropion)** of the lacrimal puncta.\"\n\n**Dry eye is the one that looks like a contradiction and is not.** A patient whose tear film is failing can present *watering*. The book calls it reflex lacrimation; `L9` slide 9 names it outright — \"**'paradoxical watering'**\". The surface is irritated because the film is unstable, irritation drives the reflex arc, and reflex tears overwhelm the drainage. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.65 lists \"**Tearing** (the eyes become dry to an extent that reflex tearing is stimulated)\" among the *symptoms of dry eye disease* itself. This is why the evaluation of a watering eye starts by excluding hypersecretion: `L9` slide 10 — \"**History** Enquiry should be made about ocular discomfort and redness to aid in **excluding hypersecretion**\", and \"A complaint of the tears **overflowing onto the cheek** is likely to indicate drainage failure rather than hypersecretion.\"\n\n**Entropion versus ectropion, since the pair is offered deliberately.** Entropion turns the lid **in**, so lashes abrade the cornea — irritation, then reflex tears. Ectropion turns the lid **out**, so the punctum no longer sits in the tear lake and cannot take tears away — a pump and aperture failure. Opposite malpositions, opposite mechanisms, same symptom. `L9` slide 11's Fig. 3.3 photographs the drainage end of this: \"(A) Marked punctal stenosis; (B) **mild medial ectropion**; (C) conjunctivochalasis; (D) punctal obstruction by an eyelash; (E) large caruncle\", and slide 10 records that \"**Punctal abnormality is the most common cause of lacrimal drainage failure**\".\n\n⚠️ **Cross-bank, and the resemblance is misleading.** `opqb-t4-147` asks \"Defective lacrimal drainage can result in any of these conditions **EXCEPT**\" — options *chronic dacryocystitis · stenosis of lacrimal puncti · **trichiasis** · mucocele of lacrimal sac*, key **trichiasis**. That question runs the arrow the other way (what defective drainage *causes*), with a different menu and a different key. Same family, not a reprint.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Dry eye, entropion and ectropion all cause epiphora — the first two by reflex overproduction and the third by punctal malposition — so the exception is none of them.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.33-34 (Ch.4 Q24; options B-D and the key are printed on p.34, orphaned from the stem)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-25', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"Which of the following is the structure responsible for secreting the outer oily layer of the tear film?",
    options:["Meibomian glands","Lacrimal glands","Goblet cells","All of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Direct book recall.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.63, layer 1 of 3: \"**Outer oily layer: Secreted by the Meibomian glands** · Its main function is to prevent/decrease tear evaporation.\" Key **A**.\n\n**Third and last of the sibling trio** — same four options, same order, one per layer:\n\n| Layer | Source | Question | Key |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Outer oily** | **Meibomian glands** | this one | **A** |\n| Middle aqueous | Lacrimal glands | `opmcq-c4-20` | B |\n| Inner mucous | Goblet cells | `opmcq-c4-18` | C |\n\n**Where the Meibomian glands are, and why it matters clinically.** They are the vertical glands in the **tarsal plates**, opening in a row along the lid margin behind the lashes. `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 5 photographs them — \"an everted lid margin showing the row of meibomian glands through the tarsal conjunctiva\" — and slide 4's sagittal diagram labels them alongside the glands of Krause, Wolfring, Zeis and Moll.\n\nThe consequence is that **lid margin disease is oil-layer disease**, which is why blepharitis keeps reappearing in the dry-eye sections. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.64 lists \"Eyelid inflammation (**blepharitis**)\" among the causes of **evaporative** dry eye, and p.65 uses blepharitis as its worked example under \"Treatment of the cause\". The chapter also carries a framed side-box, tutorial-flagged: \"**Meibomian gland dysfunction (Posterior blepharitis)**\" (p.64). Lose the oil and the aqueous evaporates — 0.1 µm of lipid on `L9` slide 3 protecting 8 µm of water.\n\n**The distractors, briefly.** **B, lacrimal glands** — the aqueous, main plus accessory. **C, goblet cells** — the mucin. **D, all of the above** — false; three tissues, three strata, no overlap.\n\n⚠️ **Cross-bank — a strong stem match with a differently built menu.** `opqb-t4-159` asks \"Which gland produces the tear film **oily layer**?\", options *Lacrimal gland · Goblet cells · **Meibomian glands** · Glands of Krause*, key Meibomian glands. Same question and same key. The menus are not identical: Grade Gain's fourth option is the **glands of Krause** where House prints \"All of the above\", and Krause is an accessory **lacrimal** gland (aqueous), which makes GG's version marginally the harder of the two. Flagged for the hub.\n\n⚠️ **A note on where Krause is taught, since the two banks differ on whether to offer it.** \"Krause\" appears in **no part of `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.4** — the book says only \"the accessory lacrimal glands in the conjunctiva\" (p.63). It is `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 4 that names it, as a figure label the lecturer circled in red. A student who has read only the book would not recognise Grade Gain's fourth option at all.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The outer oily layer is secreted by the Meibomian glands of the tarsal plates, which is why lid margin disease produces evaporative dry eye.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.34 (Ch.4 Q25)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-26', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"Dacryoadenitis is mostly caused by which of the following organisms?",
    options:["Herpes simplex virus","Adenovirus","Epstein-Barr virus","Mumps virus"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**One sentence in the book ranks all four of these options, and it is printed as a ranking, not a list.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.68, aetiology of dacryoadenitis:\n\n> \"Infections — **Most commonly viral Epstein-Barr virus.** Less common viral causes include **adenovirus**, varicella zoster, **herpes simplex**, rhinovirus, cytomegalovirus, or **mumps**. Less commonly bacterial and rarely fungal or parasitic.\"\n\nEvery option on this menu is in that sentence. Three of them are on the \"less common\" side of it. Key **C**.\n\n| Option | Position in the book's sentence |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Herpes simplex virus** | Named among the less common viral causes |\n| **B. Adenovirus** | Named among the less common viral causes — first in that list |\n| **C. Epstein-Barr virus** | \"**Most commonly viral Epstein-Barr virus**\" |\n| **D. Mumps virus** | Named among the less common viral causes — last in that list |\n\n**This is a well-made question precisely because no option is wrong on its own terms.** All four cause dacryoadenitis; only one is commonest, and the reader has to have kept the *rank* rather than the *membership*. The menu offers no bacterial or non-infective distractor at all, so \"organisms\" is not doing discriminating work here — the whole question sits inside the viral clause.\n\n⚠️ **The lecture would lead you to the wrong option, and the reason is worth understanding.** `L10) orbit` slide 31 is the **only deck in the module that teaches acute dacryoadenitis** — `L9) lacrimal system.` does not teach it at all, and its own absence list says so: \"DACRYOADENITIS, acute or chronic, and LACRIMAL GLAND TUMOURS ... None are in this deck — they are taught in L9's sibling deck L10) orbit\". Slide 31 prints:\n\n> \"Idiopathic or due to viral (**e.g. mumps, Epstein–Barr, cytomegalovirus**)\"\n\n**Mumps is printed first**, but the list is introduced by \"e.g.\" and carries **no ranking of any kind** — it is a set of examples, not an ordering. A student revising from the deck alone, remembering the first-named organism, lands on **D**. The ranking exists in `ophthalmology.pdf` p.68 and nowhere else in the course. Measured, not assumed: grepping *dacryoadenitis* case-insensitively across all 29 cached decks returns `L10) orbit` (slide 31, the teaching) and `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` (slide 7, a photograph **label** in the trio \"Dacryoadenitis | Dacryocystitis | +ve Regurge\") — and nothing else.\n\n**Same fact, asked twice in this chapter.** `opmcq-c4-7` puts it behind a vignette and offers *Staphylococci · Streptococci · Epstein Barr virus · Thyroid eye disease*; this one strips the vignette and swaps in three rival viruses. Note the bank also changes its own spelling between the two — \"Epstein Barr\" there, \"**Epstein-Barr**\" here.\n\n⚠️ **Cross-bank:** `opqb-t4-155` keys **EBV** for the same fact, on a vignette, with the menu *Staphylococcus aureus · EBV · Herpes zoster · CMV*.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Epstein-Barr virus is the commonest cause of dacryoadenitis; adenovirus, herpes simplex and mumps are all named by the book as less common, and the only deck that teaches the topic lists mumps first without ranking it.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.34 (Ch.4 Q26)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-27', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"Which of the following is / are among the causes of dry eye disease?",
    options:["Diabetes","LASIK","Pregnancy","Contact lenses","All of the above"],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"⚠️ **Five options, A–E** — one of only two such questions in the chapter (the other is `opmcq-c4-21`). Re-read from the page to confirm E is an option.\n\n**All four are on `ophthalmology.pdf` p.64, and three of them sit within eight lines of each other.** The book's non-Sjögren aqueous-deficiency list, quoted in printed order:\n\n> \"o Allergies ... o Decreased hormones associated with aging · o **Pregnancy and associated hormonal changes** · o Thyroid eye conditions ... o Some medications ... o Chemical exposures / injuries to the eyes · o Eye surgery · o **↓ corneal sensation**: – **Contact lens use** – Neurologic conditions e.g., trigeminal nerve dysfunction – **Post refractive surgery ( LASIK** or Photorefractive Keratectomy [PRK] ): typically, transient but can become chronic in some individuals · o **Diabetes** · o Vitamin A deficiency ...\"\n\nKey **E**.\n\n| Option | Book's group | Mechanism the book implies |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Diabetes** | Non-Sjögren aqueous deficiency, listed on its own | — (the book names it without a mechanism) |\n| **B. LASIK** | Under **↓ corneal sensation**, as \"post refractive surgery\" | The corneal nerves are cut, so the reflex arc that drives secretion is interrupted. \"Typically, transient but can become chronic\" |\n| **C. Pregnancy** | Hormonal | \"Pregnancy and associated hormonal changes\" |\n| **D. Contact lenses** | Under **↓ corneal sensation** | Chronic wear blunts corneal sensation |\n| **E. All of the above** | — | **The key** |\n\n**The grouping is the teaching, and it is easy to miss.** Three of the four options are aqueous-**deficiency** causes and two of those — LASIK and contact lenses — sit under a **shared sub-heading, \"↓ corneal sensation\"**, alongside trigeminal nerve dysfunction. The book is making one point three times: **the lacrimal gland is driven by a reflex from the ocular surface, so anything that numbs the surface reduces secretion.** That also explains why `ophthalmology.pdf` p.65 lists \"Tearing\" as a *symptom* of dry eye — the reflex still works when the surface is irritated rather than numbed.\n\n**Why an \"all of the above\" is fair here.** Because the four are drawn from **different groups** of the same list — hormonal, surgical, sensory and metabolic — a reader who knows one route to dry eye will still hesitate on the others. The alternative menu, four items from one sub-heading, would have been guessable.\n\n⚠️ **This is book-only content.** `L9) lacrimal system.` teaches no aetiology of dry eye at all; its absence list records \"DRY EYE / keratoconjunctivitis sicca as a topic — its classification, aqueous-deficient vs evaporative, SJÖGREN SYNDROME, and its treatment\" as absent, with dry eye surviving as two passing phrases (slides 9 and 24). Every item in this answer is `ophthalmology.pdf` p.64.\n\n⚠️ **Cross-bank:** `opqb-t4-158` asks the narrower question — \"Which of the following causes **evaporative** dry eye?\", options *vitamin A deficiency · **facial nerve palsy** · goblet cell destruction · low Schirmer test*, key facial nerve palsy (lagophthalmos → exposure → evaporation). Different half of the same p.64 aetiology, no overlap of options. Not a match.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Diabetes, LASIK, pregnancy and contact lens wear are all on the book's aqueous-deficiency list for dry eye — LASIK and contact lenses under the shared heading of reduced corneal sensation.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.34 (Ch.4 Q27)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-28', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"Which of the following conditions contributes to dry eye disease by 2 mechanisms: decreased tear secretion and increased tear evaporation?",
    options:["Sjogren syndrome","Lagophthalmos","Infrequent blinking","Thyroid eye disease"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"⚠️ **A spelling note: the bank prints \"Sjogren\" here without the diaeresis, and \"Sjögren\" with it in `opmcq-c4-1`** — both kept as printed. `ophthalmology.pdf` uses \"Sjögren\" on p.64 and \"Sjogren's\" on p.68; the inconsistency is the sources', not the transcription's.\n\n**The question asks which condition appears on BOTH of the book's aetiological lists, and exactly one does.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.64 splits dry eye into \"1. Aqueous tear deficiency\", \"2. Evaporative dry eye disease\" and \"3. Mixed aetiologies: common\". Thyroid disease is printed in **both** of the first two:\n\n> Under **aqueous deficiency**, non-Sjögren causes: \"o **Thyroid eye conditions (can be due to evaporative element too)**\"\n> Under **evaporative**: \"• **Thyroid eye disease with lid retraction, proptosis, infrequent blinking and/or lagophthalmos**\"\n\nThe parenthesis on the first list — *\"can be due to evaporative element too\"* — is the book flagging the double mechanism itself. Key **D**.\n\n**How one disease does both.** Thyroid eye disease infiltrates the **lacrimal gland**, which reduces secretion, and it retracts the lids and pushes the globe forward, which widens the palpebral fissure and exposes more surface to the air — so more evaporates from less. The book supplies the second half explicitly, naming lid retraction, proptosis, infrequent blinking and lagophthalmos as the mechanical consequences in the same bullet.\n\n| Option | Which mechanism | Both? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Sjogren syndrome** | **Aqueous deficiency only** — \"autoimmune ... dry mucous membranes throughout the body\", with lacrimal infiltration (p.64) | No |\n| **B. Lagophthalmos** | **Evaporative only** — exposure. Named inside the thyroid bullet, and again under \"Exposure keratitis (eg., lagophthalmos)\" | No |\n| **C. Infrequent blinking** | **Evaporative only** — \"associated with staring at computer or video screens (nowadays these are the most frequent contributors to DED)\" | No |\n| **D. Thyroid eye disease** | **Both**, printed on both lists | **Yes — the key** |\n\n**B and C are the trap, and it is a deliberate one.** Both are printed *inside the thyroid bullet itself* — \"Thyroid eye disease with lid retraction, proptosis, **infrequent blinking** and/or **lagophthalmos**\". They are the **mechanisms by which** thyroid disease evaporates the film, offered as if they were rival conditions. Picking either is picking a component of the key.\n\n**A is the strongest single-mechanism answer** and would win a question about the commonest *autoimmune* cause — it is the disease `opmcq-c4-1` is built on. But the book keeps Sjögren strictly under aqueous deficiency, and adds only that \"Lacrimal gland infiltration in **sarcoidosis** can also result in reduced tear production\" — again a secretion mechanism, not an evaporative one.\n\n**A useful frame for the whole p.64 list:** *deficiency* asks what is wrong with the tap, *evaporative* asks what is wrong with the lid. Thyroid eye disease is the condition that answers both questions at once, and the book's \"3. Mixed aetiologies: common\" is its way of saying this is not rare.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Thyroid eye disease is the one condition the book lists under both aqueous deficiency and evaporative dry eye — lacrimal infiltration reduces secretion while lid retraction, proptosis and lagophthalmos increase evaporation.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.34 (Ch.4 Q28)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-29', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"What is a possible complication of acute dacryoadenitis?",
    options:["Chalazion","Abscess","Dry eye","Canaliculitis"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️ **Transcription note: this question breaks across a page.** Book p.34 ends after option \"A. Chalazion\"; **options B, C and D and the key all open book p.35**. Recovered by reading the next half-page — the fourth and last question in this chapter to break this way.\n\n⚠️ **THE KEY IS NOT SOURCED IN THIS CHAPTER, AND THAT IS WORTH SAYING PLAINLY.** `ophthalmology.pdf` pp.68–69 gives dacryoadenitis a full account — aetiology, acute and chronic features, investigations, treatment — and **lists no complications at all**. There is no complications heading in the section. Where the book *does* print abscess formation is for the neighbouring disease: acute **dacryocystitis**, p.67, \"It can progress to **abscess formation**\", with the tutorial-flagged instruction \"For abscess : incision & drainage\". `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 32 says the same of dacryocystitis (\"commonly progressing to abscess formation\") and photographs it — slide 33's Fig. 3.21 caption reads \"(A) Acute dacryocystitis; (B) **lacrimal abscess** and preseptal cellulitis\". `L9` teaches no dacryoadenitis whatever.\n\n**So the key rests on general principle, and the principle is sound.** *An acutely infected glandular structure can suppurate; a lacrimal gland abscess is a recognised complication of acute dacryoadenitis, and preseptal or orbital extension may follow. **This is not taken from the course material** — neither `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.4 nor any deck names a complication of dacryoadenitis.* Of the four options offered, it is the only one that is a plausible sequel to a suppurating gland.\n\n| Option | What it actually is | Why not |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Chalazion** | A sterile, chronic granulomatous lesion of a **Meibomian** gland in the tarsal plate | A different gland, in the lid rather than the fossa, and a chronic non-infective process. It is eyelid pathology (book ch.5), not a sequel to lacrimal gland infection |\n| **B. Abscess** | A suppurative collection in the inflamed gland | **The key** |\n| **C. Dry eye** | Reduced or unstable tear film | Reasonable-sounding — the lacrimal gland makes the aqueous layer — but ⚠️ **the book never lists dacryoadenitis among the causes of dry eye** on its p.64 aetiology page, and it never lists dry eye among dacryoadenitis's outcomes. It is a sequel neither source prints |\n| **D. Canaliculitis** | Chronic infection of the **canaliculus**, \"frequently caused by **Actinomyces israelii**\" | The far end of the system — a drainage structure, not the gland — and a primary chronic disease rather than a complication of anything. ⚠️ `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.4 does not contain the word at all; it is taught **only** by `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 31 |\n\n**C is the most defensible wrong answer** and deserves the second look. Destroy enough lacrimal gland and you do get aqueous-deficient dry eye — but the book's own aqueous-deficiency list (p.64) reaches for Sjögren, sarcoid infiltration, ageing, drugs and surgery, and never for dacryoadenitis. On the printed sources, C is unsupported in exactly the way B is; B wins because a suppurating infection produces a collection, which is the general rule the chapter applies one page earlier to the sac.\n\n**What the book does tell you to watch for instead, and it is more useful than a complication list.** p.68: dacryoadenitis \"**may be a sign of a more serious pathology underlying the inflammation**\", which is why the investigations on p.69 begin with contrast CT or MRI of the orbits and include chest X-ray for sarcoidosis and \"**Biopsy** for tissue diagnosis for atypical infections or refractory disease\". The danger the book emphasises is not the abscess; it is the neoplasm or granulomatous disease behind the swelling.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A suppurating lacrimal gland can form an abscess — a complication neither the book nor the decks print for dacryoadenitis, though both print it for acute dacryocystitis, and the book's own emphasis is on imaging for underlying pathology.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.34-35 (Ch.4 Q29; options B-D and the key are printed on p.35, orphaned from the stem)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c4-30', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"What is the appropriate initial treatment for acute dacryocystitis?",
    options:["Warm compresses only","Topical steroids","Topical and systemic antibiotics","Immediate dacryocystorhinostomy"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The book's treatment list for acute dacryocystitis is four lines long and it is ordered.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.67:\n\n> \"Treatment: ▪ **Warm compresses and nasal decongestants.** ▪ **Systemic and topical antibiotics.** ▪ Once the infection resolves, consider dacryocystorhinostomy DCR ▪ For abscess : incision & drainage (Tutorial)\"\n\n`L9) lacrimal system.` slide 32 gives the same, with one addition the book omits: \"Initial treatment involves the application of **warm compresses and oral antibiotics**. **Irrigation and probing should not be performed.** Incision and drainage may be considered if pus points and an abscess is about to drain spontaneously. **Dacryocystorhinostomy is commonly required after the acute infection has been controlled.**\"\n\nKey **C**.\n\n**Why C and not A, when compresses are genuinely first on the book's list.** Because option A says \"warm compresses **only**\", and \"only\" is the word that fails. Both sources put antibiotics in the *initial* package: the book lists them as the second bullet of the same treatment block, and `L9` names them inside the sentence beginning \"**Initial** treatment involves\". This is a bacterial infection of a closed cavity — \"most commonly staphylococcal or streptococcal\" (`L9` slide 32; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.67 says \"staphylococci or streptococci\") — and compresses alone treat the symptom, not the organism. **A is the trap for a reader who remembers only the order of the list and not its contents.**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Warm compresses only** | Half the initial package. The exclusive word breaks it |\n| **B. Topical steroids** | Wrong class entirely. Steroids appear in this chapter only for **dry eye**, \"for short durations ... the inflammatory aspect\" (p.65). Nothing in either source gives a steroid to an untreated bacterial infection |\n| **C. Topical and systemic antibiotics** | The book's own second bullet, and `L9`'s \"initial treatment\" |\n| **D. Immediate dacryocystorhinostomy** | Right operation, wrong moment — see below |\n\n**D is the option this whole topic is designed around.** DCR is indeed what an obstructed sac ultimately needs, but both sources delay it explicitly and in the same words: \"**Once the infection resolves**, consider dacryocystorhinostomy DCR\" (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.67) and \"Dacryocystorhinostomy is commonly required **after the acute infection has been controlled**\" (`L9` slide 32). Operating through infected, oedematous tissue is what the delay exists to avoid. ⚠️ This is exactly why Grade Gain's `opqb-t4-160` keys **acute dacryocystitis** as the condition that is *not* an indication for DCR. Contrast `opmcq-c4-16` in this same chapter, where the disease is **chronic** and the key is **immediate DCR** — no infection to settle, so nothing to wait for.\n\n**Two instructions worth carrying that the option menu never mentions.** `L9` slide 32: \"**Irrigation and probing should not be performed**\", and slide 12: \"In acute dacryocystitis, **palpation is painful and should be avoided**\" — so the regurge test that diagnoses the chronic form is not done here. And incision and drainage is reserved, not routine: only \"if pus points and an abscess is about to drain spontaneously\".\n\n⚠️ **Cross-bank:** `opqb-t4-154` gives the same clinical picture — \"painful, red swelling below the medial canthus, very tender, with fever\" — and asks best immediate management, keying \"**Systemic antibiotics + warm compress**\". Grade Gain **combines** into one option what House splits across A and C, so the two questions are answered by the same paragraph but have differently shaped menus. Flagged for the hub. The vignette itself is House's `opmcq-c4-17`.\n\n**Chapter note:** this is the last question of chapter 4. Book p.35 carries only this one and is then blank; the red banner \"5. Eyelids\" heads book p.36.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Acute dacryocystitis is treated initially with warm compresses AND topical plus systemic antibiotics; DCR is deferred until the infection has resolved, and irrigation, probing and palpation are avoided.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.35 (Ch.4 Q30)' }
